WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.576
Detective Fix introduced himself at the Bombay police headquarters.

2
00:00:05.577 --> 00:00:10.905
He had hurried there first thing in the morning, as soon as the Mongolia had docked.

3
00:00:10.906 --> 00:00:16.623
He inquired if the warrant for Fogg's arrest had arrived from London yet, but it had not.

4
00:00:16.624 --> 00:00:25.817
"If the Mongolia were not two days ahead of schedule, I wouldn’t be in this situation," Fix explained to the Bombay director of police.

5
00:00:25.818 --> 00:00:32.215
He urged the director to issue an order for the arrest of Mr. Fogg, but the director refused.

6
00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:39.231
"The matter concerns the London office," the director explained, "and only London can deliver the order."

7
00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:43.225
Fix fumed, but he did not insist.

8
00:00:43.226 --> 00:00:50.611
"Bombay is far enough from London so that a fleeing bank robber will not worry about being seen," he thought.

9
00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:56.883
So Mr. Fogg would probably stay in Bombay for a while, and the warrant would arrive before his departure.

10
00:00:56.884 --> 00:01:01.906
In the meantime Fix was determined not to lose sight of his mysterious suspect.

11
00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:05.354
He hurried through the crowded streets back to the boat.

12
00:01:05.355 --> 00:01:08.884
He wanted to be there when Mr. Fogg disembarked.

13
00:01:08.885 --> 00:01:12.325
Mr. Fogg, for his part, left the steamer in the afternoon.

14
00:01:12.326 --> 00:01:18.546
After saying good-bye to his whist partners, he descended the gangway, carrying the carpetbag in his hand.

15
00:01:18.547 --> 00:01:27.490
On the shore he instructed Passepartout to attend to several errands, but urged him to be at the train station at eight o’clock and not a minute late. Then,

16
00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:33.961
with steps as regular as the ticking of a watch, Mr. Fogg walked to the passport office. After

17
00:01:33.962 --> 00:01:41.732
receiving a visa, he turned his steps to the train station, where he planned to have dinner and wait for the eight o’clock train to Calcutta. Passepartout,

18
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:48.313
like the detective, had thought his master would stay in Bombay at least for a few days. When

19
00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:55.203
he heard his master’s orders to meet at the train station, he realized that they were going to continue without delay. He

20
00:01:55.204 --> 00:02:02.799
purchased the shirts and shoes his master had requested and took a leisurely stroll through the strange sights of Bombay. Many

21
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.155
nationalities crowded the streets: Persians

22
00:02:05.156 --> 00:02:14.707
with pointed caps, long-robed Armenians, Indians wearing turbans, and many others in bright robes of red and orange. In

23
00:02:14.708 --> 00:02:21.916
a market he saw a procession of dancers clothed in rose-colored gowns looped with gold and silver. They danced

24
00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:26.666
airily through the streets to the sound of tambourines. Passepartout had

25
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:30.760
never seen anything like them before. Passepartout followed

26
00:02:30.761 --> 00:02:36.159
the dancers through winding streets and lanes. After a

27
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.068
while he realized that his curiosity had led him farther from the center of the city than he had intended. When he

28
00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:50.738
turned back to head to the train station, he noticed a magnificent Indian temple on nearby Malabar Hill. It was

29
00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:57.655
too splendid to pass up, and he climbed the hill, seized with a desire to see its interior. Passepartout, however,

30
00:02:57.656 --> 00:03:03.787
did not realize that it was forbidden for Christians to enter certain Indian temples, and that

31
00:03:03.788 --> 00:03:07.864
even the faithful must leave their shoes outside. The British

32
00:03:07.865 --> 00:03:13.800
government severely punished anyone who disregarded the practices of India’s religions. Meaning no

33
00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:22.060
harm, Passepartout went in like a simple tourist and was soon lost in admiration of the incredible ornamentation. "I have

34
00:03:22.061 --> 00:03:29.782
never seen anything so magnificent!" he was thinking when suddenly he was knocked to the stone floor. He looked

35
00:03:29.783 --> 00:03:35.736
up. Three angry priests jumped on him and tore off his shoes. They began

36
00:03:35.737 --> 00:03:38.946
to beat him, exclaiming loudly. "Help, help,

37
00:03:38.947 --> 00:03:44.483
they are stealing my shoes, someone help me!" screamed Passepartout. He got

38
00:03:44.484 --> 00:03:49.191
to his feet again and wasted no time in knocking down two of the priests before rushing

39
00:03:49.192 --> 00:03:53.374
out of the pagoda as fast as his legs would carry him. When Passepartout

40
00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:58.964
finally arrived at the station, he was hatless and shoeless. In the

41
00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:04.672
fight he had also lost the package with the newly purchased shirts and shoes. It was

42
00:04:04.673 --> 00:04:07.754
five minutes before eight o’clock. Fix had

43
00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:15.642
followed Mr. Fogg and was on the platform, having decided to follow him to Calcutta and farther, if necessary. Listening from

44
00:04:15.643 --> 00:04:21.652
behind a newspaper, he heard Passepartout explain his adventure to his master. Phileas Fogg was

45
00:04:21.653 --> 00:04:29.589
very annoyed by Passepartout’s misadventure, but all he said was, "I hope that this will not happen again." He turned his

46
00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.256
back and stepped onto the train. Passepartout followed his

47
00:04:32.257 --> 00:04:36.032
master without a word. Fix was about

48
00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:40.914
to enter a different train car when an idea struck him. "I’ll stay!" announced

49
00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:51.263
Fix to himself. "An offense has been committed on Indian soil. I’ve got my man!" Just then the

50
00:04:51.264 --> 00:04:59.926
locomotive gave a sharp screech, and the train moved into the darkness of the Indian night. Passepartout's Guidebook Well,

51
00:04:59.927 --> 00:05:04.024
I learned my lesson about visiting temples in India. Next

52
00:05:04.025 --> 00:05:08.909
time I travel, I'll be sure to learn more about local customs first! In

53
00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:13.263
India most people practice a religion called Hinduism. There

54
00:05:13.264 --> 00:05:19.695
are many different forms of Hinduism, but most Hindus believe in a spiritual entity called Brahman. Brahman

55
00:05:19.696 --> 00:05:23.218
is the supreme universal spirit in Hinduism. Hindus

56
00:05:23.219 --> 00:05:26.831
worship in a mandir or temple. Hinduism

57
00:05:26.832 --> 00:05:33.300
contains many distinct deities, so different mandirs are dedicated to different deities. It's

58
00:05:33.301 --> 00:05:36.081
not just people who visit these temples. Hindus

59
00:05:36.082 --> 00:05:41.914
believe all life should be treated with respect so animals are often seen hanging around too! When

60
00:05:41.915 --> 00:05:45.778
you go inside a mandir , you must remember to be respectful.

61
00:05:45.779 --> 00:05:50.443
This includes keeping your hands folded and removing your shoes before you enter.

62
00:05:50.444 --> 00:05:56.192
Oh, how I wish I had known that one!

